Add a unit test with `userIds: [self, otherUserNotInTeams]` that expects FORBIDDEN, and make sure it fails on the current code.

`PermissionRepository.getTeamIdsWithPermission` accepts `orgId` in its signature but calls `this.getTeamIdsWithPermissions({ userId, permissions: [permission], fallbackRoles })` without it. The handler calls `permissionCheckService.getTeamIdsWithPermission({ ..., orgId: user.orgId ?? undefined })`, which forwards to this method, so the org scope is discarded before any SQL runs.

::code{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="211-222"}

The effect is that an org admin's booking list now includes members from every organization that user belongs to. The old code scoped the membership condition to `user.orgId`, so this is a scope regression. It is hidden because the integration tests call the plural `getTeamIdsWithPermissions` directly, which does take `orgId`.

`getTeamIdsWithPermission` and `getTeamIdsWithPermissions` wrap the repository call in `try/catch`, log the error, and return `[]`. That was low-stakes when this was only a helper. The booking handler now depends on it for visibility. A transient DB error therefore produces the same result as "no permissions", with no signal to the caller beyond a log line. For a booking list the failure mode is that members' bookings silently disappear rather than an error being shown.

::code{file="packages/features/pbac/services/permission-check.service.ts" lines="332-341"}

Fail-closed is the right default for authorization, but the handler should be able to tell an empty result from a failure. Either let the error propagate from the booking path, or return a discriminated result. At minimum, add a test that asserts the handler surfaces a failure instead of quietly returning fewer bookings.
